#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074600} :activity/id #uuid "405276f7-9827-5091-903e-512c9c2ab67a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049418 :topic/id #uuid "f344db4d-49a0-59ae-b874-a20de566b228" :topic/math-academy-id 485} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074600 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075050 :tutorial/content "In the coordinate plane, the **midpoint** of a line segment with endpoints $(x_1,y_1)$ and $(x_2,y_2)$ lies halfway between the $x$-coordinates $x_1$ and $x_2$, and halfway between the $y$-coordinates $y_1$ and $y_2$.\n\nThe coordinates of the midpoint can be computed using the formula\n\n$$\n(x_m,y_m) = \\left(\\dfrac{x_1 +x_2}2,\\dfrac{y_1+y_2}2\\right)\n$$\n\nFor example, the coordinates of the midpoint of a line segment with endpoints $(2,1)$ and $(6,13)$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x_{m}, y_{m}) &= (\\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2}, \\frac{y_{1} + y_{2}}{2}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{2 + 6}{2}, \\frac{1 + 13}{2}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{8}{2}, \\frac{14}{2}) \\\\\n&= (4, 7)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "164a0bd6-c234-5906-82c5-f00aefe19504" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8897 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "eecc366e-853e-52a8-bc68-b16fc245ecce" :step/math-academy-id 154975 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074601}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074601 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073713 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073483 :question/id #uuid "68fc6ab1-0c7b-50b2-b541-137be5f5572e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14356"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92c49ea0-7ec5-5ffc-8a36-c4f46f78fda7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050517}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233630}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Midpoint of a Segment"} :step/id #uuid "ee2327aa-02a2-5e9b-b07c-07c22671f4fc" :step/math-academy-id 154976 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074602}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074602 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073714 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073484 :question/id #uuid "ca03fd37-a09e-5546-95a5-12f0cd1657c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-14358"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58e2e40e-209b-5bc7-98df-11841c2bbcc3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233637} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342069}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Midpoint of a Segment With Variable Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "a966a4b4-4e42-5a47-812c-3a495619e03c" :step/math-academy-id 154979 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074603}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074603 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075051 :tutorial/content "Suppose $M(4,2)$ is the midpoint between $A(1,1)$ and $B(a,b)$. What are the values of $a$ and $b$?\n\n![](images/d9/d946b98f81c7f30ff5a277c337be47dc743de531e3b138795d4a15f34ad82d54.png)\n\nAgain, we use the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x_{m}, y_{m}) &= (\\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2}, \\frac{y_{1} + y_{2}}{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} &= 1, & y_{1}|= 1, \\\\ x_{2}|= a, & y_{2}|= b, \\\\ x_{m}|= 4, & y_{m}|= 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values in the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(4, 2) &= (\\frac{1 + a}{2}, \\frac{1 + b}{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the $x$-coordinates equal to each other and the $y$-coordinates equal to each other, we have the following two equations:\n\n$$\n{4 = \\frac{1 + a}{2}; 2 = \\frac{1 + b}{2}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equations by $2$, we have:\n\n$$\n{8 = 1 + a; 4 = 1 + b\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve both equations independently:\n\n$$\n{a = 8 - 1 = 7; b = 4 - 1 = 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=7$ and $b=3$." :tutorial/id #uuid "34ecfb6d-a13d-5bc2-b346-787701d55aad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8900 :tutorial/title "Finding the Coordinates of Endpoints"} :step/id #uuid "4b327d3b-e449-5f4a-9b7f-318819c5309f" :step/math-academy-id 154983 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074604}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074604 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073715 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073485 :question/id #uuid "04e5232d-7f2a-5dd7-b08e-666c5cf1aba5" :question/math-academy-id "e-14360"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b6b70eb-df57-5d17-a753-645ccdf94945" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048760}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275140}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of an Endpoint Given the Midpoint"} :step/id #uuid "039589a4-f68f-5959-bc9d-32aaa65805e0" :step/math-academy-id 154984}] :activity/title "Midpoints in the Coordinate Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073922}]}
